oldslibc - the libc objects of the segmented program /bin/sld, one directory per libc object (name of the member of slibc.a),
one assembler source per revision: <object>/0_1.s. The revision number 0_1 is invented (other programs of the WEGA 3.1 root
will carry other revisions of the same objects; they get their own file next to it).

Origin: the libc part of /bin/sld (0x8682..0x9730) is a sequence of members of slibc.a in the order the original linker took
them. host/tools/oldslibc_cut.py cuts every object from /bin/sld (boundaries = sizes of the members), names the entry points
as the global text symbols of the member (_exit, __flsbuf, _fflush, __cleanup, _fclose, _swap, _longswap ...), writes the sources
and verifies each of them: assembled with cas -u the code bytes equal those of sld (relocated words masked). The tool also checks
that the code of every object equals the member of /lib/slibc.a (relocated words masked): all 41 objects do, so these are not
older revisions but the shipped ones (the earlier assumption "old revision of slibc" was wrong). ffltioc, getpid and umask are
not cut (6 to 8 bytes): src/cmd/ld/Makefile takes them from slibc.a.

Link order (address order): ffltioc 8682, ungetc 8688, filbuf 86fa, flsbuf 880e (__flsbuf, _fflush, __cleanup, _fclose),
__callex 8a48, cuexit 8a50 (_exit), atoi 8a5c, cnvt 8ad0, decimal 8be8, length 8c86, malloc 8cb4 (_malloc, _free, _realloc,
_calloc, _cfree), isatty 91ea, mktemp 9210, stty 9296, strchr 92c6, strrchr 92f8, swab 932e, rindex 9376, strcat 93ac,
strncmp 93e8, strcpy 943c, swap 946e (_swap, _longswap, _swapsegt), access 952c, tolower 953e, chmod 9564, close 9576,
creat 9586, fstat 9596, getpid 95aa, ioctl 95b0, link 95c8, lseek 95da, mkseg 95ee, open 95fe, read 9610, ssgbrk 9622,
signal 9638, umask 96f2, unlink 96fa, write 970a, cerror 971c..9730. The rest up to 0x9800 is zero padding of the code segment.
The own code ends at 0x8682 (sld_own.s); the `ret` at 0x8680 is its last instruction (end of the function at 0x85f8).

Jumps and calls: a target inside the object is a label L<addr>; a target in another object is its symbol name.
No fixed code address is left in the code (also lda of a table: lda rr4,L9696(r3)).
Data: the data words the code refers to are in the objects (.data, labels _dXXXX = start offset of the span, the code refers to
_dXXXX+off); tool host/tools/libc_data_seg.py. References behind the data area (bss of segment 1, offset >= 0x1BB2) stay
absolute as in the original. Not exact yet: the data of the members (atoi 24 bytes, cnvt 18, malloc 74, filbuf 20, mktemp 2,
cerror 4) is cut by the spans the code refers to, the switch table address of atoi (immediate #%01001A36) and the code
addresses in the jump tables are still absolute numbers, and the structures of data.o (__iob, __sobuf, __lastbuf) appear as
_d1959 and _d1A32 in flsbuf. The link (make link in src/cmd/ld) has the right code; the 78 differing bytes are data addresses.
`make linkslibc` links the same program with the members of slibc.a (110 differing bytes: the data layout).
